            <ArticleTitle>Inhibitory effect of Multiglycosidorum tripterygii on coronary arteriosclerosis after heart transplantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1446-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Graft coronary arteriosclerosis (GCA) is the major limiting factor for long-term survival after heart transplantation. In this study, we investigated the effect of Multiglycosidorum tripterygii (MT) on GCA and platelet-derived growth factor A (PDGF-A) mRNA expression of transplanted hearts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two groups of Lewis rats (n=7/group) underwent heterotopic heart transplantation from Wistar-King donors and were treated with either cyclosporine (CsA;10 mg/kg/day) or MT (30 mg/kg/ day). Histological evaluations of rejection and coronary arteriosclerosis, as well as Northern blot analysis on graft PDGF-A mRNA expression were made 60 days after transplantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Morphometric results indicated no significant difference in rejection between the CsA- and MT-treated groups. However, the extent of GCA in the MT-treated group was significantly less than that seen in the CsA-treated group (P&lt;0.01). The expression of PDGF-A mRNA of cardiac allograft was also significantly suppressed in the MT-treated group when compared with the CsA-treated group (P&lt;0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">MT is superior to CsA in preventing graft coronary arteriosclerosis, and this efficacy is probably associated with the depressed expression of graft PDGF-A mRNA in the MT-treated group.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
